Guideline: updates on the management of severe acute malnutrition in infants and children: This includes especially children with signs of dehydration
but no history of watery diarrhoea, children with hypothermia or hypoglycaemia, and children
with both oedema and signs of dehydration;
n in case of shock with lethargy or unconsciousness, intravenous rehydration should begin
immediately, using 15 mL/kg/h of one of the recommended fluids;
n it is important that the child is carefully monitored every 5–10 min for signs of overhydration and
signs of congestive heart failure. If signs of overhydration and congestive heart failure develop,
intravenous therapy should be stopped immediately;
n if a child with severe acute malnutrition presenting with shock does not improve after 1 h of
intravenous therapy, a blood transfusion (10 mL/kg slowly over at least 3 h) should be given;
n children with severe acute malnutrition should be given blood if they present with severe
anaemia, i.e. Hb <4 g/dL or <6 g/dL if with signs of respiratory distress;
 GUIDELINE: UPDATES ON THE MANAGEMENT OF SEVERE ACUTE MALNUTRITION IN INFANTS AND CHILDREN
n blood transfusions should only be given to children with severe acute malnutrition within the
first 24 h of admission.

